Id: aud010w
Severity: WARN
Category: logging
Check: check_accounting
Fix: Create the accounting file and enable the service (systemctl enable --now acct, or psacct), so accton runs at boot.
References: accton(8); acct(5)
Controls: CIS 8.2, 8.5; NIST AU-2, AU-12; ISO 8.15

Process accounting is installed (accton is there) but is not doing its
job: its file is missing (no /var/log/account/pacct, /var/account/pacct
or /var/log/pacct), or, on the running system, the file has not been
written to in a day although every process that exits should add a
record, or no service turns accounting on at boot (no acct.service or
psacct.service enabled, no rc link, no OpenRC runlevel), so a reboot
silences it. Whoever installed it wanted the record of who ran what;
nothing is keeping it.

Debian and Ubuntu enable acct.service and create
/var/log/account/pacct when the package is installed; Fedora and RHEL
leave psacct.service for the administrator to enable, so a psacct
installed and forgotten is the usual case. Create the file ('touch
/var/log/account/pacct'), enable the service ('systemctl enable --now
acct', or psacct), and 'lastcomm' shows records within a minute.
